@noindent
@code{cons_cell} builds new list structure by creating new pairs, while
@code{set_car} and @code{set_cdr} modify existing pairs.  Indeed, we could
implement @code{cons} in terms of the two mutators, together with an operation
that returns a new pair that is not part of any existing list structure; in
this runtime that operation is @code{cons_cell} called with two empty slots.
We obtain the new pair, set its @code{car} and @code{cdr} pointers to the
designated objects, and return the new pair as the result of the
call.@footnote{Allocating that fresh cell is one of the operations that must be
implemented as part of the memory management required by a Lisp
implementation.  We will discuss this in @ref{5.3.1}.}

@example rust
fn cons(x: Value, y: Value) -> Value @{
    let new = cons_cell(Value::Nil, Value::Nil);
    set_car(&new, x);
    set_cdr(&new, y);
    Value::Pair(new)
@}
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.12}Exercise 3.12:} The following function for
appending lists was introduced in @ref{2.2.1}:

@example rust
fn append(x: &Value, y: &Value) -> Value @{
    match x @{
        Value::Pair(cell) => @{
            let rest = append(&cell.cdr.borrow(), y);
            Value::Pair(cons_cell(cell.car.borrow().clone(), rest))
        @}
        _ => y.clone(),
    @}
@}
@end example

@code{Append} forms a new list by successively @code{cons}-ing the elements of
@code{x} onto @code{y}.  The function @code{append_bang} is similar to
@code{append}, but it is a mutator rather than a constructor.  It appends the
lists by splicing them together, modifying the final pair of @code{x} so that
its @code{cdr} is now @code{y}.  (It is an error to call @code{append_bang}
with an empty @code{x}.)

@example rust
fn append_bang(x: &Pair, y: &Pair) -> Pair @{
    set_cdr(&last_pair(x), Value::Pair(Pair::clone(y)));
    Pair::clone(x)
@}
@end example

Here @code{last_pair} is a function that returns the last pair in its
argument:

@example rust
fn last_pair(x: &Pair) -> Pair @{
    let mut current = Pair::clone(x);
    loop @{
        let next = current.cdr.borrow().clone();
        match next @{
            Value::Pair(cell) => current = cell,
            _ => return current,
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

Consider the interaction

@example rust
let x = Value::list(vec![Value::sym("a"), Value::sym("b")]);
let y = Value::list(vec![Value::sym("c"), Value::sym("d")]);
let z = append(&x, &y);

println!("@{z@}");
// => (a b c d)

println!("@{@}", cdr(&x)?);
// @r{⟨response⟩}

let w = append_bang(&first_pair(&x), &first_pair(&y));

println!("@{@}", Value::Pair(w));
// => (a b c d)

println!("@{@}", cdr(&x)?);
// @r{⟨response⟩}
@end example

What are the missing @code{⟨}@var{response}@code{⟩}s?  Draw box-and-pointer
diagrams to explain your answer.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.13}Exercise 3.13:} Consider the following
@code{make_cycle} function, which uses the @code{last_pair} function defined
in @ref{Exercise 3.12}:

@example rust
fn make_cycle(x: &Pair) @{
    set_cdr(&last_pair(x), Value::Pair(Pair::clone(x)));
@}
@end example

Draw a box-and-pointer diagram that shows the structure @code{z} created by

@example rust
let z = Value::list(vec![Value::sym("a"), Value::sym("b"), Value::sym("c")]);
make_cycle(&first_pair(&z));
@end example

What happens if we try to compute @code{last_pair(&first_pair(&z))}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.14}Exercise 3.14:} The following function is quite
useful, although obscure:

@example rust
fn mystery(x: &Pair) -> Pair @{
    fn loop_(x: Option<Pair>, y: Option<Pair>) -> Option<Pair> @{
        let Some(cell) = x else @{
            return y;
        @};
        let temp = match cell.cdr.borrow().clone() @{
            Value::Pair(next) => Some(next),
            _ => None,
        @};
        *cell.cdr.borrow_mut() = match y @{
            Some(previous) => Value::Pair(previous),
            None => Value::Nil,
        @};
        loop_(temp, Some(cell))
    @}
    match loop_(Some(Pair::clone(x)), None) @{
        Some(cell) => cell,
        None => Pair::clone(x),
    @}
@}
@end example

@code{Loop_} uses the ``temporary'' variable @code{temp} to hold the old value
of the @code{cdr} of @code{x}, since the assignment on the next line destroys
the @code{cdr}.  Explain what @code{mystery} does in general.  Suppose
@code{v} is defined by

@example rust
let v = Value::list(vec![
    Value::sym("a"),
    Value::sym("b"),
    Value::sym("c"),
    Value::sym("d"),
]);
@end example

Draw the box-and-pointer diagram that represents the list to which @code{v} is
bound.  Suppose that we now evaluate

@example rust
let w = mystery(&first_pair(&v));
@end example

Draw box-and-pointer diagrams that show the structures @code{v} and @code{w}
after evaluating this expression.  What would be printed as the values of
@code{v} and @code{w}?
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sharing and identity

We mentioned in @ref{3.1.3} the theoretical issues of ``sameness'' and
``change'' raised by the introduction of assignment.  These issues arise in
practice when individual pairs are @newterm{shared} among different data
objects.  For example, consider the structure formed by

@example rust
let x = Value::list(vec![Value::sym("a"), Value::sym("b")]);
let z1 = cons_cell(x.clone(), x);
@end example

@noindent
As shown in @ref{Figure 3.16}, @code{z1} is a pair whose @code{car} and
@code{cdr} both point to the same pair @code{x}.  This sharing of @code{x} by
the @code{car} and @code{cdr} of @code{z1} is a consequence of the
straightforward way in which @code{cons_cell} is implemented.  In general,
using @code{cons_cell} to construct lists will result in an interlinked
structure of pairs in which many individual pairs are shared by many different
structures.

