@noindent
In this structure, the pairs in the two @code{(a b)} lists are distinct,
although the actual symbols are shared.@footnote{The two pairs are distinct
because each call to @code{cons_cell} returns a new pair.  The symbols are
shared; @code{Value::sym("a")} yields equal symbols wherever the name
@code{"a"} appears, and since this edition provides no way to mutate a symbol,
this sharing is undetectable.  Note also that the sharing of pairs is what
enables us to compare them with @code{eq_pair}, which simply checks equality of
pointers.}

@noindent
When thought of as a list, @code{z1} and @code{z2} both represent ``the same''
list, @code{((a b) a b)}.  In general, sharing is completely undetectable if we
operate on lists using only @code{cons_cell} and the two selectors.  However,
if we allow mutators on list structure, sharing becomes significant.  As an
example of the difference that sharing can make, consider the following
function, which modifies the @code{car} of the structure to which it is
applied:

@example rust
fn set_to_wow(x: &Pair) -> Pair @{
    let car_value = x.car.borrow().clone();
    if let Value::Pair(inner) = car_value @{
        set_car(&inner, Value::sym("wow"));
    @}
    Pair::clone(x)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Even though @code{z1} and @code{z2} are ``the same'' structure, applying
@code{set_to_wow} to them yields different results.  With @code{z1}, altering
the @code{car} also changes the @code{cdr}, because in @code{z1} the @code{car}
and the @code{cdr} are the same pair.  With @code{z2}, the @code{car} and
@code{cdr} are distinct, so @code{set_to_wow} modifies only the @code{car}:

@example rust
println!("@{@}", Value::Pair(Pair::clone(&z1)));
// => ((a b) a b)

println!("@{@}", Value::Pair(set_to_wow(&z1)));
// => ((wow b) wow b)

println!("@{@}", Value::Pair(Pair::clone(&z2)));
// => ((a b) a b)

println!("@{@}", Value::Pair(set_to_wow(&z2)));
// => ((wow b) a b)
@end example

@noindent
One way to detect sharing in list structures is to use the predicate
@code{eq_pair}, this edition's spelling of the @code{eq?} of @ref{2.3.1}.
More generally, @code{eq_pair(&x, &y)} tests whether @code{x} and @code{y} are
the same object (that is, whether @code{x} and @code{y} are equal as pointers).
Thus, with @code{z1} and @code{z2} as defined in @ref{Figure 3.16} and
@ref{Figure 3.17}, the two halves of @code{z1} satisfy @code{eq_pair} while the
two halves of @code{z2} do not:

@example rust
println!("@{@}", Value::Bool(eq_pair(
    &first_pair(&z1.car.borrow().clone()),
    &first_pair(&z1.cdr.borrow().clone()),
)));
// => #t

println!("@{@}", Value::Bool(eq_pair(
    &first_pair(&z2.car.borrow().clone()),
    &first_pair(&z2.cdr.borrow().clone()),
)));
// => #f
@end example

As will be seen in the following sections, we can exploit sharing to greatly
extend the repertoire of data structures that can be represented by pairs.  On
the other hand, sharing can also be dangerous, since modifications made to
structures will also affect other structures that happen to share the modified
parts.  The mutation operations @code{set_car} and @code{set_cdr} should be
used with care; unless we have a good understanding of how our data objects are
shared, mutation can have unanticipated results.@footnote{The subtleties of
dealing with sharing of mutable data objects reflect the underlying issues of
``sameness'' and ``change'' that were raised in @ref{3.1.3}.  We mentioned
there that admitting change to our language requires that a compound object
must have an ``identity'' that is something different from the pieces from
which it is composed.  In Lisp, we consider this ``identity'' to be the quality
that is tested by @code{eq?}, i.e., by equality of pointers; this edition's
version is the equality of @code{Rc} handles that @code{eq_pair} tests, and it
rests on the same stipulation: a data object ``itself'' is the information
stored in some particular set of memory locations in the computer.  This
suffices for simple programs, but is hardly a general way to resolve the issue
of ``sameness'' in computational models.}

