@subsubheading Mutation is just assignment

When we introduced compound data, we observed in @ref{2.1.3} that pairs can be
represented purely in terms of functions; the dispatch closure again travels
inside a @code{ProcPair} newtype:

@example rust
fn procedural_cons(x: Value, y: Value) -> ProcPair @{
    let car_cell = RefCell::new(x);
    let tail_cell = RefCell::new(y);
    ProcPair @{
        dispatch: Rc::new(move |request| match request @{
            PairRequest::Car => car_cell.borrow().clone(),
            PairRequest::Cdr => tail_cell.borrow().clone(),
            // The book's else arm reports an undefined operation.
            other => panic!("Undefined operation: CONS: @{other:?@}"),
        @}),
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The same observation is true for mutable data.  We can implement mutable data
objects as functions using assignment and local state.  For instance, we can
extend the above pair implementation to handle @code{set_car} and
@code{set_cdr} in a manner analogous to the way we implemented bank accounts
using @code{make_account} in @ref{3.1.1}:

@example rust
fn procedural_cons(x: Value, y: Value) -> ProcPair @{
    let car_cell = RefCell::new(x);
    let tail_cell = RefCell::new(y);
    ProcPair @{
        dispatch: Rc::new(move |request| match request @{
            PairRequest::Car => car_cell.borrow().clone(),
            PairRequest::Cdr => tail_cell.borrow().clone(),
            PairRequest::SetCar(v) => @{
                *car_cell.borrow_mut() = v;
                Value::sym("done")
            @}
            PairRequest::SetCdr(v) => @{
                *tail_cell.borrow_mut() = v;
                Value::sym("done")
            @}
        @}),
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The book's application of a pair to a message, @code{(z 'car)}, is here a call
of the dispatch through @code{send}: @code{z.send(PairRequest::Car)} reads the
@code{car} slot, and @code{z.send(PairRequest::SetCar(v))} changes it.  With
the two mutator arms in place the @code{match} no longer needs the catch-all:
the compiler can see that every @code{PairRequest} is answered, exactly as it
could for the account's @code{send} in @ref{3.1.1}.

Assignment is all that is needed, theoretically, to account for the behavior of
mutable data.  As soon as we admit assignment to our language, we raise the
issues of assignment and, with them, the issues of mutable data in
general.@footnote{On the other hand, from the viewpoint of implementation,
assignment asks us to change captured state, and captured state is itself a
mutable data structure.  Thus, assignment and mutation are equipotent: Each can
be implemented in terms of the other.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.20}Exercise 3.20:} @emph{SICP exercise 3.20 is
about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the
number and teaches the same section material.}  Suppose we evaluate the
following sequence against the dispatch implementation of pairs given above:

@example rust
let x = procedural_cons(Value::int(1), Value::int(2));
let alias = ProcPair::clone(&x);

alias.send(PairRequest::SetCar(Value::int(17)));

x.send(PairRequest::Car)
// => Int(17)
@end example

@noindent
Trace this sequence through the dispatch closures above: which captured state
does each @code{send} consult, and why does @code{x.send(PairRequest::Car)}
answer 17 after the change went through @code{alias}?  What does a fresh
@code{procedural_cons(Value::int(1), Value::int(2))} answer to the same
request, and what does that contrast say about where the state of a procedural
pair lives?  (Compare @ref{Exercise 3.11}.)
@end quotation

@node	3.3.2, 3.3.3, 3.3.1, 3.3
@subsection Representing Queues

The mutators @code{set_car} and @code{set_cdr} enable us to use pairs to
construct data structures that cannot be built with @code{cons_cell},
@code{car}, and @code{cdr} alone.  This section shows how to use pairs to
represent a data structure called a queue.  Section @ref{3.3.3} will show how
to represent data structures called tables.

A @newterm{queue} is a sequence in which items are inserted at one end (called
the @newterm{rear} of the queue) and deleted from the other end (the
@newterm{front}).  @ref{Figure 3.18} shows an initially empty queue in which
the items @code{a} and @code{b} are inserted.  Then @code{a} is removed,
@code{c} and @code{d} are inserted, and @code{b} is removed.  Because items are
always removed in the order in which they are inserted, a queue is sometimes
called a @newterm{FIFO} (first in, first out) buffer.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.18}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.

@example
Operation                Resulting Queue
(define q (make-queue))
(insert-queue! q 'a)     a
(insert-queue! q 'b)     a b
(delete-queue! q)        b
(insert-queue! q 'c)     b c
(insert-queue! q 'd)     b c d
(delete-queue! q)        c d
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.18,,119mm,Figure 3.18: Queue operations.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.18.std,,119mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.}
@end float

@noindent
In terms of data abstraction, we can regard a queue as defined by the following
set of operations:

@itemize @bullet

@item
a constructor: @code{Queue::new()} returns an empty queue (a queue containing
no items).

@item
two selectors:

@example rust
⟨@var{queue}⟩.is_empty()
@end example

@noindent
tests if the queue is empty.

@example rust
⟨@var{queue}⟩.front()
@end example

@noindent
returns the object at the front of the queue, signaling an error if the queue
is empty; it does not modify the queue.

@item
two mutators:

@example rust
⟨@var{queue}⟩.insert(⟨@var{item}⟩);
@end example

@noindent
inserts the item at the rear of the queue, updating the queue in place.

@example rust
⟨@var{queue}⟩.delete();
@end example

@noindent
removes the item at the front of the queue, signaling an error if the queue is
empty before the deletion.

@end itemize

@noindent
Because a queue is a sequence of items, we could certainly represent it as an
ordinary list; the front of the queue would be the @code{car} of the list,
inserting an item in the queue would amount to appending a new element at the
end of the list, and deleting an item from the queue would just be taking the
@code{cdr} of the list.  However, this representation is inefficient, because
inserting an item requires scanning the list until we reach the end.  Since
the only method we have for scanning a list is by successively following the
@code{cdr} field, this scanning requires @math{{\Theta(n)}} steps for a list of @math{n}
items.  A simple modification to the list representation overcomes this
disadvantage by allowing the queue operations to be implemented so that they
require @math{{\Theta(1)}} steps; that is, so that the number of steps needed is
independent of the length of the queue.

The difficulty with the list representation arises from the need to scan to
find the end of the list.  The reason we need to scan is that, although the
standard way of representing a list as a chain of pairs readily provides us
with a pointer to the beginning of the list, it gives us no easily accessible
pointer to the end.  The modification that avoids the drawback is to represent
the queue as a list, together with an additional pointer that indicates the
final pair in the list.  That way, when we go to insert an item, we can consult
the rear pointer and so avoid scanning the list.

@noindent
A queue is represented, then, as a pair of pointers, @code{front} and
@code{rear}, which indicate, respectively, the first and last pairs in an
ordinary list.  Since we would like the queue to be an identifiable object, we
combine the two pointers in one @code{Queue} value.  Thus, the queue itself is
the struct that holds the two pointers.  @ref{Figure 3.19} illustrates this
representation.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.19}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+-------------------+
       +-|-+---+                   |
         |                         |
         | front-ptr               | rear-ptr
         V                         V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |
     +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.19b,,118mm,Figure 3.19: Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.19b.std,,118mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.}
@end float

To define the queue operations we start from the representation, which keeps
the front and rear pointers in two fields so that every operation can select
and modify them directly:

@example rust
struct Queue @{
    front: RefCell<Option<Pair>>,   // the front pointer
    rear: RefCell<Option<Pair>>,    // the rear pointer
@}
@end example

@noindent
Reading @code{queue.front.borrow()} selects the front pointer, and assigning
through @code{queue.front.borrow_mut()} modifies it; the rear pointer works the
same way.  The @code{Option} marks the empty list: a pointer with no pair to
point at.

@noindent
Now we can implement the actual queue operations as methods on this struct.
We will consider a queue to be empty if its front pointer is unset:

@example rust
fn is_empty(&self) -> bool @{
    self.front.borrow().is_none()
@}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{Queue::new} constructor returns, as an initially empty queue, a
@code{Queue} whose front and rear pointers are both unset:

@example rust
fn new() -> Self @{
    Queue @{
        front: RefCell::new(None),
        rear: RefCell::new(None),
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To select the item at the front of the queue, we return the @code{car} of the
pair indicated by the front pointer:

@example rust
fn front(&self) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> @{
    match self.front.borrow().clone() @{
        Some(pair) => Ok(pair.car.borrow().clone()),
        None => Err(SchemeError::UserRaised @{
            message: "front called with an empty queue".into(),
            irritants: vec![],
        @}),
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To insert an item in a queue, we follow the method whose result is indicated in
@ref{Figure 3.20}.  We first create a new pair whose @code{car} is the item to
be inserted and whose @code{cdr} is the empty list.  If the queue was initially
empty, we set the front and rear pointers of the queue to this new pair.
Otherwise, we modify the final pair in the queue to point to the new pair, and
also set the rear pointer to the new pair.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.20}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{(insert-queue! q 'd)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+--------------------------------+
       +-|-+---+                                |
         |                                      |
         | front-ptr                            | rear-ptr
         V                                      V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |        | d |
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.20c,,141mm,Figure 3.20: Result of using (insert-queue! q 'd) on the queue of Figure 3.19.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.20c.std,,141mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{(insert-queue! q 'd)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.}
@end float

@example rust
fn insert(&self, item: Value) @{
    let new_pair = cons_cell(item, Value::Nil);
    let mut rear = self.rear.borrow_mut();
    match rear.as_ref() @{
        Some(rear_pair) => set_cdr(rear_pair, Value::Pair(Rc::clone(&new_pair))),
        None => *self.front.borrow_mut() = Some(Rc::clone(&new_pair)),
    @}
    *rear = Some(new_pair);
@}
@end example

@noindent
To delete the item at the front of the queue, we merely modify the front
pointer so that it now points at the second item in the queue, which can be
found by following the @code{cdr} pointer of the first item (see
@ref{Figure 3.21}):@footnote{If the first item is the final item in the queue, the front
pointer will be unset after the deletion, which will mark the queue as
empty; we needn't worry about updating the rear pointer, which will still point
to the deleted item, because @code{is_empty} looks only at the front
pointer.}

@example rust
fn delete(&self) -> Result<(), SchemeError> @{
    let front = self.front.borrow().clone();
    let Some(pair) = front else @{
        return Err(SchemeError::UserRaised @{
            message: "delete! called with an empty queue".into(),
            irritants: vec![],
        @});
    @};
    let rest = pair.cdr.borrow().clone();
    *self.front.borrow_mut() = match rest @{
        Value::Pair(cell) => Some(cell),
        Value::Nil => None,
        other => @{
            return Err(SchemeError::TypeMismatch(format!(
                "queue node with a non-list tail: @{other@}"
            )));
        @}
    @};
    Ok(())
@}
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.21}
@ifinfo
