@subsection Representing Tables

When we studied various ways of representing sets in @ref{Chapter 2}, we
mentioned in @ref{2.3.3} the task of maintaining a table of records
indexed by identifying keys.  In the implementation of data-directed
programming in @ref{2.4.3}, we made extensive use of two-dimensional
tables, in which information is stored and retrieved using two keys.  Here we
see how to build tables as mutable list structures.

We first consider a one-dimensional table, in which each value is stored under
a single key.  We implement the table as a list of records, each of which is
implemented as a pair consisting of a key and the associated value. The records
are glued together to form a list by pairs whose @code{car}s point to
successive records.  These gluing pairs are called the @newterm{backbone} of
the table.  To have a place that we can change when we add a new
record to the table, we build the table as a @newterm{headed list}.  A headed
list has a special backbone pair at the beginning, which holds a dummy
``record''---in this case the arbitrarily chosen symbol @code{*table*}.
In this edition that headed list is the section's @code{Table}: the marker
cell, with the record list hanging from its @code{cdr}.
@ref{Figure 3.22} shows the box-and-pointer diagram for the table

@example rust
// Table::new builds the headed cell; each insert splices a record
// in after the marker.
let table = Table::new();
table.insert(Value::sym("a"), Value::int(1));
table.insert(Value::sym("b"), Value::int(2));
table.insert(Value::sym("c"), Value::int(3));
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.22}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.22:} A table represented as a headed list.

@example
+---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
+-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
|            |            |            |
V            V            V            V
+---------+   +---+---+   +---+---+   +---+---+
| *table* |   | * | * |   | * | * |   | * | * |
+---------+   +-|-+-|-+   +-|-+-|-+   +-|-+-|-+
|   |       |   |       |   |
V   V       V   V       V   V
+---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+
| a | | 1 | | b | | 2 | | c | | 3 |
+---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.22c,,138mm,Figure 3.22: A table represented as a headed list.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.22c.std,,138mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.22:} A table represented as a headed list.}
@end float

@noindent
To extract information from a table we use the table's @code{lookup}
operation, which takes a key as argument and returns the associated value
(or false, the absent arm of the lookup's @code{Option}, if there is no
value stored under that key).  @code{Lookup} is defined in terms of the
@code{assoc} operation, which expects a key and a list of records as
arguments.  Note that @code{assoc} never sees the dummy record.
@code{Assoc} returns the record that has the given key as its
@code{car}, or @code{None} if there is no such record.@footnote{Because
keys are compared with the table's structural test, @code{assoc} can
recognize keys that are symbols, numbers, or list structure, as the
book's @code{equal?}-based test does.}  @code{Lookup} then matches on
the @code{Option} returned by @code{assoc} and returns the value (the
@code{cdr}) of the record when there is one.

@example rust
impl Table @{
    // The book's `assoc`: the first record whose key satisfies the
    // table's key test, or `None`.
    pub fn assoc(&self, key: &Value, records: &Value) -> Option<Pair> @{
        let same_key = Rc::clone(&self.same_key);
        assoc_by(key, records, move |a, b| same_key(a, b))
    @}

    // The book's `lookup`: the value stored under `key`, or `None`.
    pub fn lookup(&self, key: &Value) -> Option<Value> @{
        let records = self.header().cdr.borrow().clone();
        self.assoc(key, &records)
            .map(|record| record.cdr.borrow().clone())
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To insert a value in a table under a specified key, we first use @code{assoc}
to see if there is already a record in the table with this key.  If not, we
form a new record by pairing the key with the value, and insert this at
the head of the table's list of records, after the dummy record.  If there
already is a record with this key, we set the @code{cdr} of this record to the
designated new value.  The header of the table provides us with a fixed
location to modify to insert the new record.@footnote{Thus, the first
backbone pair is the object that represents the table ``itself''; that is, a
clone of the table's header handle points at this same pair.  This same
backbone pair always starts the table.  If we did not arrange things in this
way, @code{insert} would have to return a new value for the start of the table
when it added a new record.}

@example rust
impl Table @{
    // The book's `insert!`: replaces the value of an existing record
    // or splices a new one at the front of the record list.
    pub fn insert(&self, key: Value, value: Value) @{
        let header = self.header();
        let records = header.cdr.borrow().clone();
        match self.assoc(&key, &records) @{
            Some(record) => set_cdr(&record, value),
            None => set_cdr(
                &header,
                Value::Pair(cons_cell(Value::Pair(cons_cell(key, value)), records)),
            ),
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To construct a new table, we simply build the headed cell: the marker symbol
in the car, the record list hanging empty from its cdr.  That is the cell
@code{Table::new} built for the session above, and the constructor installs
the table's key comparison with it, the structural test
@code{structural_same_key}.  A table that should recognize its keys some
other way takes the caller's own predicate instead, which is the design
exercise 3.24 asks for.  The operations just defined then work over any
table built this way:

@example rust
table.lookup(&Value::sym("b"))
// => 2

table.lookup(&Value::sym("d"))
// => #f

table.insert(Value::sym("b"), Value::int(20));

table.lookup(&Value::sym("b"))
// => 20
@end example

@subsubheading Two-dimensional tables

In a two-dimensional table, each value is indexed by two keys.  We can
construct such a table as a one-dimensional table in which each key identifies
a subtable.  @ref{Figure 3.23} shows the box-and-pointer diagram for the table

@example
math:  +: 43    letters:  a: 97
       -: 45              b: 98
       *: 42
@end example

@noindent
which has two subtables.  (The subtables don't need a special header symbol,
since the key that identifies the subtable serves this purpose.)

