@noindent
Having defined @code{full_adder} as a function, we can now use it as a
building block for creating still more complex circuits.  (For example, see
@ref{Exercise 3.30}.)

In essence, our simulator provides us with the tools to construct a language of
circuits.  If we adopt the general perspective on languages with which we
approached the study of programming in @ref{1.1}, we can say that the
primitive function boxes form the primitive elements of the language, that
wiring boxes together provides a means of combination, and that specifying
wiring patterns as functions serves as a means of abstraction.

@subsubheading Primitive function boxes

The primitive function boxes implement the ``forces'' by which a change in the
signal on one wire influences the signals on other wires.  To build function
boxes, we use the following operations on wires:

@itemize @bullet

@item @code{⟨@var{wire}⟩.signal()}

@noindent
returns the current value of the signal on the wire.

@item @code{⟨@var{wire}⟩.set_signal(⟨@var{new value}⟩)}

@noindent
changes the value of the signal on the wire to the new value.

@item @code{⟨@var{wire}⟩.add_action(&⟨@var{action}⟩)}

@noindent
asserts that the designated action should be run whenever the signal on the
wire changes value.  Such actions are the vehicles by which changes in the
signal value on the wire are communicated to other wires.

@end itemize

@noindent
In addition, we will make use of a method @code{after_delay} that takes a
time delay and an action to be run and schedules the given action to run
after the given delay.

Using these operations, we can define the primitive digital logic functions.
To connect an input to an output through an inverter, we use @code{add_action}
to associate with the input wire an action that will be run whenever the
signal on the input wire changes value.  The action computes the
@code{logical_not} of the input signal, and then, after one
@code{INVERTER_DELAY}, sets the output signal to be this new value:

@example rust
pub fn inverter(input: &Wire, output: &Wire, agenda: &Rc<Agenda>) @{
    // The book's `invert-input`, as a closure the input wire holds.
    // A wire is a cheap handle: a clone shares the same state.
    let input = input.clone();
    let output = output.clone();
    let agenda = Rc::clone(agenda);
    let action: Rc<dyn Fn()> = Rc::new(move || @{
        let new_value = logical_not(input.signal());
        let output = output.clone();
        agenda.after_delay(INVERTER_DELAY, Rc::new(move || @{
            output.set_signal(new_value);
        @}));
    @});
    input.add_action(&action);
@}

pub fn logical_not(s: u8) -> u8 @{
    u8::from(s == 0)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The book's @code{logical-not} signals an error for a signal other than 0 or
1.  Here the signal is a byte that the section's discipline keeps at 0 or 1,
so no other value can reach the conversion, and @code{u8::from} maps 0 to 1
and 1 to 0.

@noindent
An and-gate is a little more complex.  The action procedure must be run if
either of the inputs to the gate changes.  It computes the @code{logical_and}
(using a function analogous to @code{logical_not}) of the values of the
signals on the input wires and sets up a change to the new value to occur on
the output wire after one @code{AND_GATE_DELAY}.

@example rust
pub fn and_gate(a1: &Wire, a2: &Wire, output: &Wire, agenda: &Rc<Agenda>) @{
    // The book's `and-action-procedure`; one closure serves both inputs.
    let a1 = a1.clone();
    let a2 = a2.clone();
    let output = output.clone();
    let agenda = Rc::clone(agenda);
    let and_action: Rc<dyn Fn()> = Rc::new(move || @{
        let new_value = logical_and(a1.signal(), a2.signal());
        let output = output.clone();
        agenda.after_delay(AND_GATE_DELAY, Rc::new(move || @{
            output.set_signal(new_value);
        @}));
    @});
    a1.add_action(&and_action);
    a2.add_action(&and_action);
@}
@end example

@quotation
