@subsubheading Representing wires

A wire in our simulation will be a computational object with two local state
variables: a @code{signal} (initially taken to be 0) and a collection of
@code{actions} to be run when the signal changes value.  We implement the wire
as a struct that holds this state behind an @code{Rc}, with the changes made
through @code{Cell} and @code{RefCell} and the local operations exposed as
methods, just as we did with the simple bank-account object in
@ref{3.1.1}:

@example rust
struct WireState @{
    signal: Cell<u8>,
    actions: RefCell<Vec<Rc<dyn Fn()>>>,
@}

#[derive(Clone)]
pub struct Wire @{
    state: Rc<WireState>,
@}

impl Wire @{
    /// The book's `make-wire`: signal 0, no actions.
    pub fn new() -> Self @{
        Wire @{
            state: Rc::new(WireState @{
                signal: Cell::new(0),
                actions: RefCell::new(Vec::new()),
            @}),
        @}
    @}

    /// The book's `get-signal`.
    pub fn signal(&self) -> u8 @{
        self.state.signal.get()
    @}

    /// The book's `set-signal!`.
    pub fn set_signal(&self, value: u8) @{
        if self.state.signal.get() == value @{
            return;
        @}
        self.state.signal.set(value);
        let snapshot: Vec<_> = self.state.actions.borrow().clone();
        for action in snapshot @{
            action();
        @}
    @}

    /// The book's `add-action!`: the action joins the wire's list and
    /// then runs once immediately.
    pub fn add_action(&self, action: &Rc<dyn Fn()>) @{
        self.state.actions.borrow_mut().insert(0, Rc::clone(action));
        action();
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The local method @code{set_signal} tests whether the new signal value
changes the signal on the wire.  If so, it stores the new value and runs each
of the actions, with a @code{for} loop over a snapshot of the list; the
snapshot matters because an action may attach further actions to the same
wire while it runs, and the loop must not walk a list that is changing under
it.@footnote{Observe that
the @code{if} in this method has no @code{else}.  Such a ``one-armed
@code{if}'' is used to decide whether to do something, rather than to select
between two expressions: when the new value equals the old, the method simply
returns.  In Rust an @code{if} without an @code{else} has the type
@code{()}, the value it yields when the predicate is false.}

The method @code{add_action} adds the given action to the list of actions to
be run, and then runs the new action once.  (See @ref{Exercise 3.31}.)

The three methods just shown are the wire's whole outward face; the state
record behind them stays private to the @code{Wire} handles that share
it.@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 155} These methods are simply the wire's local
procedures under stable names, so ordinary method-call syntax reaches the
operations the state record keeps private.  It is striking that we can
interchange the role of ``procedures'' and ``data'' in such a simple way.
The actions a wire stores are data, an ordinary vector of closures that we
can count and clone, yet calling one runs it; and the wire itself, a value we
pass by handle, answers requests like @code{signal()} and
@code{set_signal(1)} as if it were a procedure being applied.  The truth of
the matter is that, in a language in which we can deal with procedures as
objects, there is no fundamental difference between ``procedures'' and
``data,'' and we can choose our names to allow us to program in whatever
style we choose.}

@noindent
Wires, which have time-varying signals and may be incrementally attached to
devices, are typical of mutable objects.  We have modeled them as a shared
state record whose fields are modified through @code{Cell} and
@code{RefCell}, the interior mutability the bank account's balance used in
@ref{3.1.1}.  When a new wire is created, a new state record is allocated
(in @code{Wire::new}) and a fresh @code{Wire} handle over it is constructed
and returned.

The wires are shared among the various devices that have been connected to
them.  Thus, a change made by an interaction with one device will affect all
the other devices attached to the wire.  The wire communicates the change to
its neighbors by calling the action procedures provided to it when the
connections were established.

@subsubheading The agenda

The only thing needed to complete the simulator is @code{after_delay}.  The
idea here is that we maintain a data structure, called an @newterm{agenda},
that contains a schedule of things to do.  The agenda is an explicit value in
this edition: every gate and probe receives the same @code{Rc<Agenda>} handle
when it is constructed.  The following operations are defined for agendas:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Agenda::new()} returns a new empty agenda.

@item
@code{⟨@var{agenda}⟩.is_empty()} is true if the specified agenda is
empty.

@item
@code{⟨@var{agenda}⟩.current_time()} returns the current simulation time.

@item
@code{⟨@var{agenda}⟩.add_to(⟨@var{time}⟩, ⟨@var{action}⟩)}
modifies the agenda by adding the given action to be run at the
specified time.

@item
@code{⟨@var{agenda}⟩.propagate()} runs the agenda down: it executes the first
scheduled item, removes it, and keeps going as long as items remain.

@end itemize

@noindent
The particular agenda that a circuit runs on is shared by every box attached
to it.  The method @code{after_delay} adds new elements to it:

@example rust
impl Agenda @{
    /// The book's `after-delay`: schedules `action` `delay` from now.
    pub fn after_delay(self: &Rc<Self>, delay: u64, action: Rc<dyn Fn()>) @{
        self.add_to(self.current_time() + delay, action);
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The receiver is an @code{Rc<Agenda>} rather than a plain borrow because the
scheduled actions capture the agenda themselves: an action that runs later
must still hold the shared schedule it came from.

The simulation is driven by the method @code{propagate}, which operates on
the agenda, executing each action on the agenda in sequence.  In
general, as the simulation runs, new items will be added to the agenda, and
@code{propagate} will continue the simulation as long as there are items on the
agenda:

@example rust
impl Agenda @{
    /// The book's `propagate`.
    pub fn propagate(&self) @{
        loop @{
            // The earliest entry is the one with the least (time, seq).
            let next = self.heap.borrow_mut().pop();
            let Some(item) = next else @{
                break; // nothing scheduled: the simulation is done
            @};
            let scheduled = item.0;
            self.now.set(scheduled.time);
            (scheduled.action)();
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading A sample simulation

The following function, which places a ``probe'' on a wire, shows the
simulator in action.  The probe tells the wire that, whenever its signal
changes value, it should record the new signal value, together with the
current time and a name that identifies the wire, in a shared log; the log
lines below appear in the order they were recorded:

@example rust
pub fn probe_wire(name: &str, wire: &Wire, agenda: &Rc<Agenda>, log: &ProbeLog) @{
    let name = name.to_string();
    let wire = wire.clone();
    let agenda = Rc::clone(agenda);
    let log = Rc::clone(log);
    let action: Rc<dyn Fn()> = Rc::new(move || @{
        log.borrow_mut().push(format!(
            "@{name@} @{@}  New-value = @{@}",
            agenda.current_time(),
            wire.signal()
        ));
    @});
    wire.add_action(&action);
@}
@end example

@noindent
A probe runs once as it is attached, which is why each wire's starting value
already shows at time 0.  We begin by creating the agenda.  The delays for
the primitive function boxes are constants of the section, with the book's
values: @code{INVERTER_DELAY} is 2, @code{AND_GATE_DELAY} is 3, and
@code{OR_GATE_DELAY} is 5:

@example rust
let agenda = Rc::new(Agenda::new());
@end example

@noindent
Now we define four wires, placing probes on two of them:

@example rust
let input_1 = Wire::new();
let input_2 = Wire::new();
let sum = Wire::new();
let carry = Wire::new();
let log = probe_log();

probe_wire("sum", &sum, &agenda, &log);
// sum 0  New-value = 0

probe_wire("carry", &carry, &agenda, &log);
// carry 0  New-value = 0
@end example

@noindent
Next we connect the wires in a half-adder circuit (as in @ref{Figure 3.25}),
set the signal on @code{input_1} to 1, and run the simulation:

@example rust
half_adder(&input_1, &input_2, &sum, &carry, &agenda);

input_1.set_signal(1);
agenda.propagate();
// sum 8  New-value = 1
@end example

@noindent
The @code{sum} signal changes to 1 at time 8.  We are now eight time units from
the beginning of the simulation.  At this point, we can set the signal on
@code{input_2} to 1 and allow the values to propagate:

@example rust
input_2.set_signal(1);
agenda.propagate();
// carry 11  New-value = 1
// sum 16  New-value = 0
@end example

@noindent
The @code{carry} changes to 1 at time 11 and the @code{sum} changes to 0 at
time 16.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.31}Exercise 3.31:} The method @code{add_action}
defined on @code{Wire} specifies that when a new action procedure is added to
a wire, the procedure is immediately run.  Explain why this initialization is
necessary.  In particular, trace through the half-adder example in the
paragraphs above and say how the system's response would differ if the
action were attached without the immediate run, as the library's
@code{add_action_deferred} does:

@example rust
pub fn add_action_deferred(&self, action: &Rc<dyn Fn()>) @{
    self.state.actions.borrow_mut().insert(0, Rc::clone(action));
@}
@end example
@end quotation

@subsubheading Implementing the agenda

Finally, we give details of the agenda data structure, which holds the
actions that are scheduled for future execution.

The agenda is a priority queue of @newterm{scheduled actions}.  Each entry is
a record with three fields: the time at which the action runs, a sequence
number that records when the action was scheduled, and the action itself.
Entries at the same time keep their scheduling order, so one entry's time and
sequence number play the role of the book's time segment and its queue
together (see @ref{Exercise 3.32}):

@example rust
struct Scheduled @{
    time: u64,
    seq: u64,
    action: Rc<dyn Fn()>,
@}

// Ordered by (time, seq): earlier times first, and within one time,
// the order in which the actions were scheduled. The PartialEq, Eq,
// and PartialOrd impls Rust asks for alongside Ord delegate to the
// same key.
impl Ord for Scheduled @{
    fn cmp(&self, other: &Self) -> Ordering @{
        (self.time, self.seq).cmp(&(other.time, other.seq))
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The heap replaces the book's hand-built segment list: its ordering key keeps
every time's actions in scheduling order, the same first-in, first-out
discipline the queue operations described in @ref{3.3.2} enforce.

The agenda itself is the heap together with two counters.  It differs from
the tables described in @ref{3.3.3} in that the entries will be kept
in order of increasing time, earliest first, rather than looked up by key.  In
addition, we store the @newterm{current time} (i.e., the time of the last
action that was processed) in a cell of the agenda.  A newly constructed
agenda has nothing scheduled and has a current time of
0:@footnote{The book's agenda is a headed list, like the tables in
@ref{3.3.3}; since this edition keeps the current time in its own field of
the agenda, the schedule needs no header at all.}

@example rust
pub struct Agenda @{
    now: Cell<u64>,      // the current time
    next_seq: Cell<u64>, // the next sequence number to hand out
    heap: RefCell<BinaryHeap<Reverse<Scheduled>>>,
@}

impl Agenda @{
    /// The book's `make-agenda`: time 0, nothing scheduled.
    pub fn new() -> Self @{
        Agenda @{
            now: Cell::new(0),
            next_seq: Cell::new(0),
            heap: RefCell::new(BinaryHeap::new()),
        @}
    @}

    /// The book's `current-time`.
    pub fn current_time(&self) -> u64 @{
        self.now.get()
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The standard @code{BinaryHeap} pops its largest element first, so each entry
travels wrapped in @code{Reverse}, which flips the comparison and leaves the
earliest @code{(time, seq)} on top.  An agenda is empty if it has nothing
scheduled:

@example rust
impl Agenda @{
    /// The book's `empty-agenda?`.
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool @{
        self.heap.borrow().is_empty()
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To add an action to an agenda, we take the next sequence number and push the
entry onto the heap.  The ordering key does the work that the book's scan
over segments did: the entry lands after every entry that runs earlier, and
after every entry at the same time that was scheduled before it, wherever the
heap happens to keep it.

@example rust
impl Agenda @{
    /// The book's `add-to-agenda!`.
    pub fn add_to(&self, time: u64, action: Rc<dyn Fn()>) @{
        let seq = self.next_seq.get();
        self.next_seq.set(seq + 1);
        self.heap.borrow_mut().push(Reverse(Scheduled @{ time, seq, action @}));
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Removing the first item is one pop: the heap hands back the entry with the
earliest @code{(time, seq)}, and there is no segment to splice out when a
queue empties, because the heap holds entries rather than segment records.
The @code{propagate} method shown earlier is the loop that pops,
advances the current time to the entry's time, and runs the action.
Whenever we extract an item, we also update the current
time:@footnote{In this way, the current time will always be the time of the
action most recently processed.  Storing this time in its own cell of the
agenda ensures that it will still be available even if the entry that carried
it has already been popped.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.32}Exercise 3.32:} The actions to be run during one
time of the agenda are executed in the order in which they were scheduled
(first in, first out); the sequence number of a @code{Scheduled} entry is
what keeps that order.  Explain why this order must be used.  In particular,
trace the behavior of an and-gate whose inputs change from 0, 1 to 1, 0 at
the same time and say how the behavior would differ if entries scheduled for
the same time were ordered last in, first out instead, each new entry going
ahead of the ones already waiting.
@end quotation


@node	3.3.5, 3.4, 3.2, 3.3
@subsection Propagation of Constraints

Computer programs are traditionally organized as one-directional computations,
which perform operations on prespecified arguments to produce desired outputs.
On the other hand, we often model systems in terms of relations among
quantities.  For example, a mathematical model of a mechanical structure might
include the information that the deflection @math{d} of a metal rod is related to
the force @math{F} on the rod, the length @math{L} of the rod, the cross-sectional
area @math{A}, and the elastic modulus @math{E} via the equation
@displaymath
{dAE} \,=\, {FL.}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dAE = FL
@end example

@end ifinfo

Such an equation is not one-directional.  Given any four of the quantities, we
can use it to compute the fifth.  Yet translating the equation into a
traditional computer language would force us to choose one of the quantities to
be computed in terms of the other four.  Thus, a procedure for computing the
area @math{A} could not be used to compute the deflection @math{d}, even though the
computations of @math{A} and @math{d} arise from the same
equation.@footnote{Constraint propagation first appeared in the incredibly
forward-looking @abbr{SKETCHPAD} system of Ivan @ref{Sutherland (1963)}.  A
beautiful constraint-propagation system based on the Smalltalk language was
developed by Alan @ref{Borning (1977)} at Xerox Palo Alto Research Center.  Sussman,
Stallman, and Steele applied constraint propagation to electrical circuit
analysis (@ref{Sussman and Stallman 1975}; @ref{Sussman and Steele 1980}). TK!Solver
(@ref{Konopasek and Jayaraman 1984}) is an extensive modeling environment based on
constraints.}

In this section, we sketch the design of a language that enables us to work in
terms of relations themselves.  The primitive elements of the language are
@newterm{primitive constraints}, which state that certain relations hold
between quantities.  For example, @code{adder(network, a, b, c)} specifies that the
quantities @math{a}, @math{b}, and @math{c} must be related by the equation
@math{{a + b = c}}, @code{multiplier(network, x, y, z)} expresses the constraint
@math{{xy = z}}, and @code{constant(network, 3.14, x)} says that the value of
@math{x} must be 3.14.

Our language provides a means of combining primitive constraints to
express more complex relations.  We combine constraints by constructing
@newterm{constraint networks}, in which constraints are joined by
@newterm{connectors}.  A connector is an object that ``holds'' a value that may
participate in one or more constraints.  For example, we know that the
relationship between Fahrenheit and Celsius temperatures is
@displaymath
{9C} \,=\, {5(F - 32).}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
9C = 5(F - 32)
@end example

@end ifinfo

Such a constraint can be thought of as a network consisting of primitive adder,
multiplier, and constant constraints (@ref{Figure 3.28}).  In the figure, we
see on the left a multiplier box with three terminals, labeled @math{{m1}}, @math{{m2}},
and @math{p}.  These connect the multiplier to the rest of the network as follows:
The @math{{m1}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{C}, which will hold the Celsius
temperature.  The @math{{m2}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{w}, which is also
linked to a constant box that holds 9.  The @math{p} terminal, which the
multiplier box constrains to be the product of @math{{m1}} and @math{{m2}}, is linked to
the @math{p} terminal of another multiplier box, whose @math{{m2}} is connected to a
constant 5 and whose @math{{m1}} is connected to one of the terms in a sum.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.28}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{9C = 5(F - 32)} expressed as a constraint network.

@example
       +---------+     +---------+   v   +---------+
C -----+ m1      |  u  |      m1 +-------+ a1      |
       |    *  p +-----+ p  *    |       |    *  s +---- F
    +--+ m2      |     |      m2 +--+ +--+ a2      |
    |  +---------+     +---------+  | |  +---------+
  w |                              x| |y
    |    +-----+        +-----+     | |     +-----+
    +----+  9  |        |  5  +-----+ +-----+  32 |
         +-----+        +-----+             +-----+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.28,,142mm,Figure 3.28: The relation 9C = 5(F - 32) expressed as a constraint network.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.28.std,,142mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{{9C = 5(F - 32)}} expressed as a constraint network.}
@end float


@noindent
Computation by such a network proceeds as follows: When a connector is given a
value (by the user or by a constraint box to which it is linked), it awakens
all of its associated constraints (except for the constraint that just awakened
it) to inform them that it has a value.  Each awakened constraint box then
polls its connectors to see if there is enough information to determine a value
for a connector.  If so, the box sets that connector, which then awakens all of
its associated constraints, and so on.  For instance, in conversion between
Celsius and Fahrenheit, @math{w}, @math{x}, and @math{y} are immediately set by the
constant boxes to 9, 5, and 32, respectively.  The connectors awaken the
multipliers and the adder, which determine that there is not enough information
to proceed.  If the user (or some other part of the network) sets @math{C} to a
value (say 25), the leftmost multiplier will be awakened, and it will set @math{u}
to @math{{25 \cdot 9 = 225}}.  Then @math{u} awakens the second multiplier, which sets @math{v} to
45, and @math{v} awakens the adder, which sets @math{f} to 77.

@subsubheading Using the constraint system

To use the constraint system to carry out the temperature computation outlined
above, we first create two connectors, @code{c} and @code{f}, by calling the
constructor @code{Connector::new}, and link @code{c} and @code{f} in an
appropriate network:

@example rust
let network = Network::new();
let c = Connector::new();
let f = Connector::new();
celsius_fahrenheit_converter(&network, &c, &f);
@end example

@noindent
The function that creates the network is defined as follows:

@example rust
pub fn celsius_fahrenheit_converter(
    network: &Network,
    c: &Connector,
    f: &Connector,
) @{
    network.adopt(c);
    network.adopt(f);
    let u = network.connector();
    let v = network.connector();
    let w = network.connector();
    let x = network.connector();
    let y = network.connector();
    multiplier(&network, c, &w, &u);
    multiplier(&network, &v, &x, &u);
    adder(&network, &v, &y, f);
    constant(&network, 9.0, &w);
    constant(&network, 5.0, &x);
    constant(&network, 32.0, &y);
@}
@end example

@noindent
This function creates the internal connectors @code{u}, @code{v}, @code{w},
@code{x}, and @code{y}, and links them as shown in @ref{Figure 3.28} using the
primitive constraint constructors @code{adder}, @code{multiplier}, and
@code{constant}.  The two named connectors were created outside the network, so
the converter first asks the network to @code{adopt} each of them: the network
is the strong root that keeps every connector and constraint of the graph
alive.  Just as with the digital-circuit simulator of
@ref{3.3.4}, expressing these combinations of primitive elements in terms of
functions automatically provides our language with a means of abstraction for
compound objects.

To watch the network in action, we can place probes on the connectors @code{c}
and @code{f}, using a probe similar to the one we used to monitor wires in
@ref{3.3.4}.  Placing a probe on a connector will append a line to a shared
log whenever the connector is given a value:

@example rust
let log = probe_log();
probe_connector(&network, "Celsius temp", &c, &log);
probe_connector(&network, "Fahrenheit temp", &f, &log);
@end example

@noindent
Next we set the value of @code{c} to 25.  (The second argument to
@code{set_value} tells @code{c} that this directive comes from the
@code{user}.)

@example rust
let user = Informant::user();
c.set_value(25.0, &user)
    .expect("a fresh connector accepts its first value");
log.borrow().as_slice()
// => ["Probe: Celsius temp = 25", "Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 77"]
@end example

@noindent
The probe on @code{c} awakens and records the value.  @code{c} also propagates
its value through the network as described above.  This sets @code{f} to 77,
which the probe on @code{f} records.

Now we can try to set @code{f} to a new value, say 212:

@example rust
let refused = match f.set_value(212.0, &user) @{
    Ok(()) => panic!("F must refuse 212 while C holds 25"),
    Err(error) => error,
@};
println!("@{refused@}");
// => Contradiction (77 212)
@end example

@noindent
The connector complains that it has sensed a contradiction: Its value is 77,
and someone is trying to set it to 212.  If we really want to reuse the network
with new values, we can tell @code{c} to forget its old value:

@example rust
c.forget_value(&user);
log.borrow().as_slice()
// => ["Probe: Celsius temp = 25", "Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 77",
//     "Probe: Celsius temp = ?", "Probe: Fahrenheit temp = ?"]
@end example

@noindent
@code{c} finds that the @code{user}, who set its value originally, is now
retracting that value, so @code{c} agrees to lose its value, as shown by the
log, and informs the rest of the network of this fact.  This information
eventually propagates to @code{f}, which now finds that it has no reason for
continuing to believe that its own value is 77.  Thus, @code{f} also gives up
its value, as shown by the log.

Now that @code{f} has no value, we are free to set it to 212:

@example rust
f.set_value(212.0, &user)
    .expect("a forgotten connector accepts a fresh value");
log.borrow().as_slice()
// => ["Probe: Celsius temp = 25", "Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 77",
//     "Probe: Celsius temp = ?", "Probe: Fahrenheit temp = ?",
//     "Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 212", "Probe: Celsius temp = 100"]
@end example

@noindent
This new value, when propagated through the network, forces @code{c} to have a
value of 100, and this is recorded by the probe on @code{c}.  Notice that the
very same network is being used to compute @code{c} given @code{f} and to
compute @code{f} given @code{c}.  This nondirectionality of computation is the
distinguishing feature of constraint-based systems.

@subsubheading Implementing the constraint system

The constraint system is implemented via objects with local state, in a manner
very similar to the digital-circuit simulator of
@ref{3.3.4}.  Here the objects are structs behind @code{Rc} handles, with
their local state in @code{RefCell}s.  Although the primitive objects of the
constraint system are somewhat more complex, the overall system is simpler,
since there is no concern about agendas and logic delays.

The basic operations on connectors are the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.has_value()} tells whether the connector has a value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.value()} returns the connector's current value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.set_value(⟨@var{new-value}⟩, ⟨@var{informant}⟩)}
indicates that the informant is requesting the connector to set its value to
the new value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.forget_value(⟨@var{retractor}⟩)} tells the connector
that the retractor is requesting it to forget its value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.connect(⟨@var{new-constraint}⟩)} tells the connector
to participate in the new constraint.

@end itemize

@noindent
The connectors communicate with the constraints through the @code{Constraint}
trait: calling @code{inform_about_value} on a constraint tells it that the
connector has a value, and calling @code{inform_about_no_value} tells the
constraint that the connector has lost its value.

@code{Adder} constructs an adder constraint among summand connectors
@code{a1} and @code{a2} and a @code{sum} connector.  An adder is implemented as
a struct with local state; the struct together with its own @code{Informant} is
this edition's version of the book's @code{me} object, and the constructor
connects the new adder to the designated connectors, registers it so the
network owns it, and returns it:

@example rust
struct Adder @{
    a1: Weak<ConnectorState>,
    a2: Weak<ConnectorState>,
    sum: Weak<ConnectorState>,
    me: Informant,
@}

pub fn adder(
    network: &Network,
    a1: &Connector,
    a2: &Connector,
    sum: &Connector,
) -> Rc<dyn Constraint> @{
    let constraint = Rc::new(Adder @{
        a1: weak_connector(a1),
        a2: weak_connector(a2),
        sum: weak_connector(sum),
        me: Informant::new(),
    @});
    let object: Rc<dyn Constraint> = constraint.clone();
    a1.connect(&object);
    a2.connect(&object);
    sum.connect(&object);
    network.register(object);
    constraint
@}
@end example

@noindent
In the book, the adder's @code{me} procedure acts as a dispatch to the local
procedures, and two ``syntax interfaces'' (see @ref{Footnote 155} in
@ref{3.3.4}) are used in conjunction with the dispatch.  The @code{Constraint}
trait folds the dispatch and its interfaces into two methods, which a connector
calls directly:

@example rust
constraint.inform_about_value();
constraint.inform_about_no_value();
@end example

@noindent
The book's local procedures @code{process-new-value} and
@code{process-forget-value}, and the two arms of the dispatch that route the
requests to them, become the following:

@example rust
impl Adder @{
    fn process_new_value(&self) @{
        let (Some(a1), Some(a2), Some(sum)) = (
            connector_of(&self.a1),
            connector_of(&self.a2),
            connector_of(&self.sum),
        ) else @{
            return;
        @};
        let (x, y, total) = (a1.value(), a2.value(), sum.value());
        if let (Some(x), Some(y)) = (x, y) @{
            let _ = sum.set_value(x + y, &self.me);
        @} else if let (Some(x), Some(total)) = (x, total) @{
            let _ = a2.set_value(total - x, &self.me);
        @} else if let (Some(y), Some(total)) = (y, total) @{
            let _ = a1.set_value(total - y, &self.me);
        @}
    @}
@}

impl Constraint for Adder @{
    fn informant(&self) -> &Informant @{
        &self.me
    @}

    fn inform_about_value(&self) @{
        self.process_new_value();
    @}

    fn inform_about_no_value(&self) @{
        if let Some(sum) = connector_of(&self.sum) @{
            sum.forget_value(&self.me);
        @}
        if let Some(a1) = connector_of(&self.a1) @{
            a1.forget_value(&self.me);
        @}
        if let Some(a2) = connector_of(&self.a2) @{
            a2.forget_value(&self.me);
        @}
        self.process_new_value();
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The adder's local procedure @code{process_new_value} is called when the adder
is informed that one of its connectors has a value. The adder first checks to
see if both @code{a1} and @code{a2} have values. If so, it tells @code{sum} to
set its value to the sum of the two addends.  The @code{informant} argument to
@code{set_value} is @code{me}, which is the adder object itself.  If @code{a1}
and @code{a2} do not both have values, then the adder checks to see if perhaps
@code{a1} and @code{sum} have values.  If so, it sets @code{a2} to the
difference of these two.  Finally, if @code{a2} and @code{sum} have values,
this gives the adder enough information to set @code{a1}.  If the adder is told
that one of its connectors has lost a value, it requests that all of its
connectors now lose their values.  (Only those values that were set by this
adder are actually lost.)  Then it runs @code{process_new_value}.  The reason
for this last step is that one or more connectors may still have a value (that
is, a connector may have had a value that was not originally set by the adder),
and these values may need to be propagated back through the adder.

A multiplier is very similar to an adder. It will set its @code{product} to 0
if either of the factors is 0, even if the other factor is not known.  The
@code{Multiplier} struct holds @code{m1}, @code{m2}, @code{product}, and
@code{me} exactly as @code{Adder} does:

@example rust
pub fn multiplier(
    network: &Network,
    m1: &Connector,
    m2: &Connector,
    product: &Connector,
) -> Rc<dyn Constraint> @{
    let constraint = Rc::new(Multiplier @{
        m1: weak_connector(m1),
        m2: weak_connector(m2),
        product: weak_connector(product),
        me: Informant::new(),
    @});
    let object: Rc<dyn Constraint> = constraint.clone();
    m1.connect(&object);
    m2.connect(&object);
    product.connect(&object);
    network.register(object);
    constraint
@}

impl Multiplier @{
    fn process_new_value(&self) @{
        let (Some(m1), Some(m2), Some(product)) = (
            connector_of(&self.m1),
            connector_of(&self.m2),
            connector_of(&self.product),
        ) else @{
            return;
        @};
        let (x, y, total) = (m1.value(), m2.value(), product.value());
        if x == Some(0.0) || y == Some(0.0) @{
            let _ = product.set_value(0.0, &self.me);
        @} else if let (Some(x), Some(y)) = (x, y) @{
            let _ = product.set_value(x * y, &self.me);
        @} else if let (Some(total), Some(x)) = (total, x) @{
            let _ = m2.set_value(total / x, &self.me);
        @} else if let (Some(total), Some(y)) = (total, y) @{
            let _ = m1.set_value(total / y, &self.me);
        @}
    @}
@}

impl Constraint for Multiplier @{
    fn informant(&self) -> &Informant @{
        &self.me
    @}

    fn inform_about_value(&self) @{
        self.process_new_value();
    @}

    fn inform_about_no_value(&self) @{
        if let Some(product) = connector_of(&self.product) @{
            product.forget_value(&self.me);
        @}
        if let Some(m1) = connector_of(&self.m1) @{
            m1.forget_value(&self.me);
        @}
        if let Some(m2) = connector_of(&self.m2) @{
            m2.forget_value(&self.me);
        @}
        self.process_new_value();
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
A @code{constant} constructor simply sets the value of the designated
connector.  A correct network never notifies a constant box, so the book's
error for a stray message has nothing to answer: the box's two trait methods
are empty.

@example rust
pub fn constant(
    network: &Network,
    value: f64,
    connector: &Connector,
) -> Rc<dyn Constraint> @{
    struct ConstantBox(Informant);

    impl Constraint for ConstantBox @{
        fn informant(&self) -> &Informant @{
            &self.0
        @}

        fn inform_about_value(&self) @{@}

        fn inform_about_no_value(&self) @{@}
    @}

    let me = Rc::new(ConstantBox(Informant::new()));
    let object: Rc<dyn Constraint> = me.clone();
    connector.connect(&object);
    let _ = connector.set_value(value, me.informant());
    network.register(object);
    me
@}
@end example

@noindent
Finally, a probe appends a line to the log about the setting or unsetting of
the designated connector:

@example rust
pub fn probe_connector(
    network: &Network,
    name: &str,
    connector: &Connector,
    log: &ProbeLog,
) -> Rc<dyn Constraint> @{
    struct Probe @{
        name: String,
        connector: Weak<ConnectorState>,
        log: ProbeLog,
        me: Informant,
    @}

    impl Constraint for Probe @{
        fn informant(&self) -> &Informant @{
            &self.me
        @}

        fn inform_about_value(&self) @{
            let Some(connector) = self.connector.upgrade() else @{
                return;
            @};
            let probe = Connector @{ state: connector @};
            if let Some(value) = probe.value() @{
                self.log.borrow_mut().push(format!(
                    "Probe: @{@} = @{value@}", self.name));
            @}
        @}

        fn inform_about_no_value(&self) @{
            self.log.borrow_mut()
                .push(format!("Probe: @{@} = ?", self.name));
        @}
    @}

    let me = Rc::new(Probe @{
        name: name.to_string(),
        connector: weak_connector(connector),
        log: Rc::clone(log),
        me: Informant::new(),
    @});
    let object: Rc<dyn Constraint> = me.clone();
    connector.connect(&object);
    network.register(object);
    me
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Representing connectors

A connector is represented as a handle over shared local state: one
@code{Rc<ConnectorState>} whose fields are @code{RefCell}s, @code{value}, the
current value of the connector; @code{informant}, the object
that set the connector's value; and @code{constraints}, a list of the
constraints in which the connector participates.  The links to the constraints
are weak, so a connector and its constraints do not keep each other alive:

@example rust
#[derive(Default)]
struct ConnectorState @{
    value: RefCell<Option<f64>>,
    informant: RefCell<Option<Informant>>,
    constraints: RefCell<Vec<Weak<dyn Constraint>>>,
@}

#[derive(Clone, Default)]
pub struct Connector @{
    state: Rc<ConnectorState>,
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Connector::new} is the book's @code{make-connector}: a valueless
connector with no constraints.  The book's local procedures become methods on
the handle:

@example rust
impl Connector @{
    /// The book's `has-value?`: whether anyone currently owns the value.
    pub fn has_value(&self) -> bool @{
        self.state.informant.borrow().is_some()
    @}

    /// The book's `get-value`: the current value, or `None` when the
    /// connector has none.
    pub fn value(&self) -> Option<f64> @{
        if !self.has_value() @{
            return None;
        @}
        *self.state.value.borrow()
    @}

    /// The book's `set-value!`.
    pub fn set_value(&self, new_value: f64, setter: &Informant)
        -> Result<(), SchemeError>
    @{
        if !self.has_value() @{
            *self.state.value.borrow_mut() = Some(new_value);
            *self.state.informant.borrow_mut() = Some(setter.clone());
            self.notify_value_except(setter);
            return Ok(());
        @}
        let stored = *self.state.value.borrow();
        if stored == Some(new_value) @{
            return Ok(());
        @}
        let mut irritants = Vec::new();
        if let Some(old) = stored @{
            irritants.push(Value::real(old));
        @}
        irritants.push(Value::real(new_value));
        Err(SchemeError::UserRaised @{
            message: "Contradiction".into(),
            irritants,
        @})
    @}

    /// The book's `forget-value!`.
    pub fn forget_value(&self, retractor: &Informant) @{
        let owned = self
            .state
            .informant
            .borrow()
            .as_ref()
            .is_some_and(|informant| informant == retractor);
        if !owned @{
            return;
        @}
        *self.state.informant.borrow_mut() = None;
        self.notify_no_value_except(retractor);
    @}

    /// The book's `connect`.
    pub fn connect(&self, constraint: &Rc<dyn Constraint>) @{
        self.add_constraint_once(constraint);
        if self.has_value() @{
            constraint.inform_about_value();
        @}
    @}

    fn add_constraint_once(&self, constraint: &Rc<dyn Constraint>) @{
        let mut constraints = self.state.constraints.borrow_mut();
        let present = constraints.iter().any(|weak| @{
            weak.upgrade()
                .is_some_and(|known| known.informant() == constraint.informant())
        @});
        if !present @{
            constraints.insert(0, Rc::downgrade(constraint));
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The connector's local procedure @code{set_value} is called when there is a
request to set the connector's value.  If the connector does not currently have
a value, it will set its value and remember as @code{informant} the constraint
that requested the value to be set.@footnote{The @code{setter} might not be a
constraint.  In our temperature example, we used the @code{user} informant as
the setter.}  Then the connector will notify all of its participating
constraints except the constraint that requested the value to be set.  This is
accomplished using the following iterator, which applies a designated method to
all constraints in the list except a given one:

@example rust
fn each_constraint_except(
    &self,
    exception: &Informant,
) -> Vec<Rc<dyn Constraint>> @{
    self.state
        .constraints
        .borrow()
        .iter()
        .filter_map(Weak::upgrade)
        .filter(|constraint| constraint.informant() != exception)
        .collect()
@}

fn notify_value_except(&self, exception: &Informant) @{
    for constraint in self.each_constraint_except(exception) @{
        constraint.inform_about_value();
    @}
@}

fn notify_no_value_except(&self, exception: &Informant) @{
    for constraint in self.each_constraint_except(exception) @{
        constraint.inform_about_no_value();
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The list holds its members weakly, so the iterator upgrades each link as it
goes, and a constraint whose strong owner is gone quietly drops out.  When the
exception is the constraint that just set the value, the test is again a
physical comparison: each constraint answers for the @code{Informant} identity
through which it sets values, and two informants are the same exactly when they
are the same object.

If a connector is asked to forget its value, it runs the local procedure
@code{forget_value}, which first checks to make sure that the request is
coming from the same object that set the value originally, comparing the two
informants physically.  If so, the connector informs its associated
constraints about the loss of the value.

The book's @code{me} object plays two roles in the connector: it dispatches to
the local procedures, and it represents the connector as an object, with a
handful of ``syntax interface'' procedures standing in front of the dispatch.
In this edition the five operations above are the connector's public methods,
and the handle itself names the object:

@example rust
connector.has_value();
connector.value();
connector.set_value(new_value, &informant);
connector.forget_value(&retractor);
connector.connect(&new_constraint);
@end example

@noindent
The local method @code{connect} adds the designated new constraint to the
list of constraints if it is not already in that list, recognizing membership
by the @code{Informant} identity.  Then, if the connector has a value, it
informs the new constraint of this fact.

@quotation
@strong{In this language: mutable data.}

Rust makes mutation visible in the types and in the sharing.  A pair's halves
live in RefCells inside one Rc cell, so every mutator's borrow is checked at
run time, and two names share state exactly when they hold the same
Rc handle, which Rc::ptr_eq settles.  The section's queues, tables, wires, and
connectors are objects of that kind, and the gates' actions and the connectors'
constraints are held only weakly, so wiring cannot keep a gate's inputs alive;
the Network and the caller's own handles keep a system alive.

@example rust
let x = Value::list(vec![Value::sym("a"), Value::sym("b")]);
let z1 = cons_cell(x.clone(), x.clone());
set_car(&z1, Value::list(vec![Value::sym("wow"), Value::sym("b")]));
Value::Pair(z1).to_string()
// => "((wow b) wow b)"
@end example

The book's mechanism still wins when the sharing crosses code the compiler
never saw together, because which names hold the same Rc is a property of the
pointers, and only Rc::ptr_eq settles it, the role eq? plays in the text.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.33}Exercise 3.33:} Using primitive multiplier,
adder, and constant constraints, define a function @code{averager} that takes
a network and three connectors @code{a}, @code{b}, and @code{c} as inputs and
establishes the constraint that the value of @code{c} is the average of the
values of @code{a} and @code{b}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.34}Exercise 3.34:} Louis Reasoner wants to build a
squarer, a constraint device with two terminals such that the value of
connector @code{b} on the second terminal will always be the square of the
value @code{a} on the first terminal.  He proposes the following simple device
made from a multiplier:

@example rust
fn squarer(network: &Network, a: &Connector, b: &Connector) @{
    multiplier(network, a, a, b);
@}
@end example

There is a serious flaw in this idea.  Explain.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.35}Exercise 3.35:} Ben Bitdiddle tells Louis that
one way to avoid the trouble in @ref{Exercise 3.34} is to define a squarer as a
new primitive constraint.  Fill in the missing portions in Ben's outline for a
constraint to implement such a device:

@example rust
fn squarer(a: &Connector, b: &Connector) -> Rc<dyn Constraint> @{
    struct Squarer @{
        a: Connector,
        b: Connector,
        me: Informant,
    @}

    impl Constraint for Squarer @{
        fn informant(&self) -> &Informant @{
            &self.me
        @}

        fn inform_about_value(&self) @{
            // the book's process-new-value
            if let Some(v) = self.b.value() @{
                if v < 0.0 @{
                    ⟨@var{report "square less than 0: SQUARER" and give up}⟩
                @} else @{
                    ⟨@var{alternative1}⟩
                @}
            @} else @{
                ⟨@var{alternative2}⟩
            @}
        @}

        fn inform_about_no_value(&self) @{
            // the book's process-forget-value
            ⟨@var{body1}⟩
        @}
    @}

    let me = Rc::new(Squarer @{
        a: a.clone(),
        b: b.clone(),
        me: Informant::new(),
    @});
    let object: Rc<dyn Constraint> = me;
    ⟨@var{rest of definition}⟩
    object
@}
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.36}Exercise 3.36:} @emph{SICP exercise 3.36 is
about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the
number and teaches the same section material.}  Suppose we evaluate

@example rust
let a = Connector::new();
let user = Informant::user();

a.set_value(10.0, &user)
    .expect("a fresh connector accepts its first value");
@end example

@noindent
and that a logging constraint was connected to @code{a} before the last line,
one that appends a line to a transcript each time its @code{Constraint}
dispatch is consulted and each time its internal process procedure runs.  At
some moment during the evaluation of @code{a.set_value}, the connector's local
method reaches

@example rust
fn notify_value_except(&self, exception: &Informant) @{
    for constraint in self.each_constraint_except(exception) @{
        constraint.inform_about_value();
    @}
@}
@end example

Trace the calls, in order: name each dispatch of the logging constraint, the
internal @code{process_new_value} each dispatch runs, and the connector
methods that hand the requests along.  Do the same for the matching
@code{a.forget_value(&user)}, whose loop is @code{notify_no_value_except}.
Explain why the constraint is consulted even though the setter, @code{user},
never is.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.37}Exercise 3.37:} The
@code{celsius_fahrenheit_converter} function is cumbersome when compared with
a more expression-oriented style of definition, such as

@example rust
fn celsius_fahrenheit_converter(network: &Network, x: &Connector) -> Connector @{
    let scaled = c_div(
        network,
        &c_mul(network, &cv(network, 9.0), x),
        &cv(network, 5.0),
    );
    c_add(network, &scaled, &cv(network, 32.0))
@}

let network = Network::new();
let c = network.connector();
let f = celsius_fahrenheit_converter(&network, &c);
@end example

Here @code{c_add}, @code{c_mul}, etc. are the ``constraint'' versions of the
arithmetic operations.  For example, @code{c_add} takes two connectors as
arguments and returns a connector that is related to these by an adder
constraint:

@example rust
fn c_add(network: &Network, x: &Connector, y: &Connector) -> Connector @{
    let z = network.connector();
    adder(network, x, y, &z);
    z
@}
@end example

Define analogous functions @code{c_sub}, @code{c_mul}, @code{c_div}, and
@code{cv} (constant value) that enable us to define compound constraints as in
the converter example above.@footnote{The expression-oriented format is
convenient because it avoids the need to name the intermediate expressions in a
computation.  Our original formulation of the constraint language is cumbersome
in the same way that many languages are cumbersome when dealing with operations
on compound data.  For example, if we wanted to compute the product
@math{{(a + b) \cdot (c + d)}}, where the variables represent vectors, we could work
in ``imperative style,'' using functions that set the values of designated
vector arguments but do not themselves return vectors as values:

@example rust
v_sum(&a, &b, &mut temp1);
v_sum(&c, &d, &mut temp2);
v_prod(&temp1, &temp2, &mut answer);
@end example

Alternatively, we could deal with expressions, using functions that return
vectors as values, and thus avoid explicitly mentioning @code{temp1} and
@code{temp2}:

@example rust
let answer = v_prod(v_sum(&a, &b), v_sum(&c, &d));
@end example

Since Rust functions can return compound objects as values, we can transform
our imperative-style constraint language into an expression-oriented style as
shown in this exercise.  In languages that are impoverished in handling
compound objects, such as Algol, Basic, and Pascal (unless one explicitly uses
Pascal pointer variables), one is usually stuck with the imperative style when
manipulating compound objects.  Given the advantage of the expression-oriented
format, one might ask if there is any reason to have implemented the system in
imperative style, as we did in this section.  One reason is that the
non-expression-oriented constraint language provides a handle on constraint
objects (e.g., the @code{Rc<dyn Constraint>} that @code{adder} returns) as well
